<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="methylquercetin biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-6064" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: The expected taxonomic range of the pathway includes Oryza sativa Japonica Group. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; Flavonoids constitute an important group of plant secondary metabolites. Over 10,000 flavonoids have been described in the literature |CITS: [17587669]|. Flavonoids are known for their diverse bioactivities. They act as antioxidants, plant defense agent, anticancer agent etc. The structural diversity of flavonoids is attributed to acylation, glycosylation and methylation of their hydroxyl groups. O-methylation of flavonoids results in a reduction of their reactivity and an increase in their antimicrobial activity, whereas glycosylation increases their solubility and stability |CITS: [18817819]|. O-methylation of flavonoids is catalyzed by O-methyltransferases (OMTs). They transfer the methyl group from S-adenosyl-l-methionine to specific hydroxyl groups of flavonoids. Plant OMTs are highly regioselective, i. e. each one transfers the methyl group to a specific hydroxyl group in a flavonoid |CITS: [18817819]|. The 3&apos;-&lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-methyltransferase specifically &lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-methylating the B-ring 3&apos;- position of flavonoids and 3-position of phenylpropanoids has been isolated from |FRAME:TAX-36749| and heterologously expressed and partially purified in &lt;i&gt;E.coli&lt;/i&gt;. With respect to substrate specificity the two OMT homologs identified in |FRAME:TAX-36749| were both unique in the sense that they accepted phenylpropanoids and flavonoids alike, however expressed a clear preference for flavonoids over phenylpropanoids. It was proposed that these methyltransferases are more ancient or adapted to the living conditions of |FRAME:TAX-36749| as other methyltransferases have lost the capability to catalyze both compound classes |CITS:[9514654]|.</Description>
  <Authors>
    <Author name="O-28" />
    <Author name="hartmut" />
    <Author name="THE-ARABIDOPSIS-INFORMATION-RESOURCE" />
    <Author name="karthik" />
