    <DataNode elementId="MONOMER4FS-37" textLabel="Quercetin 3&apos;-O-methyltransferase" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="MONOMER4FS-37" dataSource="plantcyc" />
      <Graphics centerX="1162.22607421875" centerY="50.0" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="PMN, nilo-poyanco">Plant O-methyltransferases are known to be involved in methylation of plant secondary metabolites, especially phenylpropanoid and flavonoid compounds. An OMT, ROMT-9, was cloned and characterized from rice using a reverse transcriptase polymerase chain reaction (RT-PCR). ROMT-9 was expressed in Escherichia coli and its recombinant protein was purified using affinity chromatography. It was then tested for its ability to transfer the methyl group of S-adenosyl-L-methionine to the flavonoid substrates, eriodictyol, luteolin, quercetin, and taxifolin, all of which have a 3&apos;-hydroxyl functional group. The reaction products were analyzed using TLC, HPLC, HPLC/MS, and NMR spectroscopy. The NMR analysis showed that ROMT-9 transferred the methyl group specifically to the 3-hydroxyl group of quercetin, resulting in the formation of its methoxy derivative. Furthermore, ROMT-9 converted flavonoids containing the 3-hydroxy functional group such as eriodictyol, luteolin, quercetin and taxifolin into the corresponding methoxy derivatives, suggesting that ROMT-9 is an OMT with strict specificity for the 3-hydroxy group of flavonoids |CITS: [16412485]|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="MONOMER4FS-37" />
      <Property key="Synonym_1" value="ROMT-9" />
      <Property key="Catalyzes" value="RXN-4FS-73" />
      <Property key="Gene" value="G4FS-26" />
      <Property key="InstanceNameTemplate" value="MONOMER-*" />
      <Property key="Species" value="TAX-4530" />
      <CitationRef elementRef="citation_PUB_16412485" />
    </DataNode>
    <DataNode elementId="MONOMER-20526" textLabel="flavonoid 3&apos;,5&apos;-O-methyltransferase 1" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="Q6ZD89" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="277.49188232421875" centerY="1256.724609375" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="SRI, caspi">The |FRAME: G-58316 ROMT-9| gene of rice was the second rice &lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-methyltransferase (OMT)-encoding gene to be characterized, after the |FRAME: G-16013 NOMT| gene, which encodes |FRAME: EC-2.1.1.232 EC 2.1.1.232, naringenin 7-&lt;em&gt;O&lt;/em&gt;-methyltransferase| and participates in |FRAME: PWY-5116 sakuranetin biosynthesis| |CITS: [8651913]|. The |FRAME: G-58316 ROMT-9| gene was one of 19 genes containing a class 2 OMT domain found in the rice genome. It has the same binding site as |FRAME: EC-2.1.1.68 EC 2.1.1.68, caffeate &lt;em&gt;O&lt;/em&gt;-methyltransferase| from |FRAME: TAX-3879 &lt;i&gt;Medicago sativa&lt;/i&gt;| except for one amino-acid difference, an |FRAME: VAL L-valine| instead of an |FRAME: ILE L-isoleucine|. The gene was expressed in root, stem and leaf tissue |CITS: [16412485]|. The gene was amplified from cDNA with a His&lt;sub&gt;6&lt;/sub&gt; tag and expressed in |FRAME: TAX-562 &lt;i&gt;Escherichia coli&lt;/i&gt;|, and the recombinant protein was purified to near homogeneity. Incubation of the purified protein with several substrates showed that it methylated 3&apos;-hydroxylated flavonoids, including the flavanols |FRAME: CPD-19097 (-)-catechin|, |FRAME: MYRICETIN myricetin| and |FRAME: CPD-474 (+)-taxifolin|, the flavanone |FRAME: CPD-6994 (2S)-eriodictyol|, and the flavones |FRAME: CPD-520 quercetin| and |FRAME: 5734-TETRAHYDROXYFLAVONE luteolin|, but not on flavonoids that lacked a 3&apos;-OH group. Analysis of the products confirmed that they were methylated on the 3&apos;-OH group. Kinetic analysis determined that the enzyme prefers flavonol or flavone to flavanone substrates |CITS: [16412485]|. The enzyme was also unable to act on |FRAME: CPD-20494 tamarixetin|, (a quercetin derivative with a 4&apos; methoxy group), but could act on |FRAME: CPD-13511 rhamnetin| (a quercetin derivative with a 7-methoxy group). In that study it was reported that the enzyme could transfer only one methyl group to |FRAME: MYRICETIN myricetin| (which also contains a 5&apos;-OH group) and could not produce further methylation in other positions ad thus the enzyme was defined as a flavonoid 3&apos;-&lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-methyltransferase |CITS: [16412485]|. A subsequent study determined that |FRAME: G-58316 ROMT-9| participates in |FRAME: CPD-12572 tricin| biosynthesis in rice. A rice T-DNA insertion mutant for |FRAME: G-58316 ROMT-9| showed 46% reduction in |FRAME: CPD-12572 tricin| accumulation compared with the wild-type, while |FRAME: 5734-TETRAHYDROXYFLAVONE luteolin| and |FRAME: CPD-12571 selgin| were increased by 6 and 9.5 times, respectively. Interestingly, incubation of the purified methyltransferase with |FRAME: CPD-12571 selgin| resulted in its conversion to |FRAME: CPD-12572 tricin|, suggesting that |FRAME: G-58316 ROMT-9| also has 5&apos;-hydroxylase activity, contradictory to the previous report |CITS: [26082402]|. However, it is possible that other methyltransferases are responsible for the 5&apos;-hydroxylation reaction, as both ROMT-15 and -ROMT-17were reported to hydroxylate myricetin and tricetin on both the 3&apos; and the 5&apos; hydroxyl groups |CITS: [17943312]|. |FRAME: EC-2.1.1.267 EC 2.1.1.267, flavonoid 3&amp;prime;,5&amp;prime;-methyltransferase|</Comment>
